BEYOND THE DOCTRINE:
STORYTELLING IN PLEADING
Put yourself in the position of a plaintiffs’ lawyer. You have a potential client whose case, based on your initial investigation, is a strong one. This potential client wants you to file suit and you agree. What are your goals when you sit down to draft the complaint?

If your first thought is “comply with Rule 8(a)(2),” you need to dive a little deeper into character! What is your goal as an advocate? To win—and as a practical matter, in most cases this means convincing the defendant that it is better to pay up now with a generous settlement, rather than fight a long and expensive court battle—and then have to pay up anyway.

How can a complaint help you do this? Should your complaint be sparse or detailed? Should it overflow with legalese, or tell a story using plain words? The answer depends on your audience. Who do you need to convince of the rightness of your cause? In some cases, it is the defendant, who has to be willing to pay.

In some cases, it may be the judge, if you risk losing a motion to dismiss. And in some cases, your audience may be … your audience! Imagine you are a musician, with fans around the world, and you get into a legal dispute with other members of your group. Who do you most need on your side—the judge? Or the fans?

Before reading further, watch a short video to learn more about Storytelling in Pleading in a lawsuit between members of an electronic dance music group.

INTRODUCTORY NOTE ON ALTERNATIVE ALLEGATIONS, RULE 8(d), AND MCCORMICK V. KOPMANN
It has long been the practice, under the Federal Rules and in many state courts, for complaints to plead claims in the alternative. Pleading in the alternative takes this basic form: The defendant did X—but if not, then the defendant did Y. When the pleader is not confident as to the facts but has grounds to believe that either of two alternative narratives may be true, the complaint can say so. As you read Rule 8(d) and McCormick v. Kopmann, try to imagine different situations where a plaintiff may have no choice but to plead in the alternative. Then try to imagine a scenario where a party could, with some investigative effort, pin down a single account of what happened, rather than pleading in
